[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d2193c2a-f0fa-5d81-a54f-d064202b6613",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(R)=-11 + 10 \\sqrt{5}$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 3 & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 3 & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ 3 & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-5-6)+2(3 \\sqrt{5}- 2 \\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-11)+2\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-11+2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-11 + 2 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=-11 + 2 \\sqrt{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ef0ec340-39f5-503e-bd4d-e24a8e5efa4a",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
