    "question": {
      ":question/id": "61d37d79-4198-525a-be4c-b5b077d31497",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(D)=3 \\sqrt{5}$\n- **II.** $det(D)=2 \\sqrt{5}$\n- **III.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{5} & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{5} & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{5} & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(3-2)+0+\\frac{1}{2}(0+2 \\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=2 \\sqrt{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d4b1b553-d2ec-5c28-b459-c74af2a9c221",
