    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f8a6b904-1394-528a-8e5e-5cde3f96ff18",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(A)=3 \\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $det(A)=0$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)(0- \\sqrt{3})+(- \\sqrt{3})(- \\frac{3}{2}+3)+(-1)(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+0) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{3})+(- \\sqrt{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{3}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2d2045f4-8bb7-5da1-8e1e-7478687f8f94",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
