  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ab34efb5-995b-525a-9fde-69708474b44c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(E)=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- **III.** $det(E)=- \\frac{4}{5}$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & -3 & 1 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & 3 & - \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & -3 & 1 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & 3 & - \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & - \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & 1 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -3 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{9}{2}-3)+2(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{5})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}- \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "19439e1c-eb94-5429-99b0-5e90af3c6185",
