    "question": {
      ":question/id": "48baaf63-6abf-55dd-a8a9-d6f560d902aa",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(A)=\\frac{2}{7}$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{7} & - \\frac{1}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & \\frac{4}{5} & -3 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{5} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7} & - \\frac{1}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & \\frac{4}{5} & -3 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{5} & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -3\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{5}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}(- \\frac{12}{5}+\\frac{12}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}(- \\frac{3}{7}+0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{7}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{35} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=- \\frac{3}{35}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
