  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e1861beb-f35b-5b08-b9d2-c3a4d9662508",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(T)=\\frac{32}{3}$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+1(-2+\\frac{16}{3})+1(2+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+(-2+\\frac{16}{3})+(2+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bec1cf25-b5bd-579c-9e12-a75a7ccbb891",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
